// wrangler.jsonc - the STAGING Worker's configuration. Infrastructure, not app: GitHub Pages
// (release) never reads this file, and nothing in dct-ios.html does either.
//
// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (owner GO 2026-09-09, "html_handling none via wrangler.jsonc, no version bump"):
//   The staging Worker (https://phantom-staging.wfj6t2fk7w.workers.dev) was created from the
//   dashboard as a Worker with static assets. With no config in the repo it ran the platform
//   default html_handling of "auto-trailing-slash", which answers /dct-ios.html with a 307 to
//   /dct-ios. GitHub Pages serves /dct-ios.html directly. The two surfaces must answer the same
//   path the same way, or a staging look is not a look at the release path: the service worker
//   precaches 'dct-ios.html' through that redirect and stores a response flagged redirected, and
//   an offline navigation answered from such an entry is rejected by the browser.
//   "none" means exact paths only - what GitHub Pages does. Measured after deploy; the numbers
//   are in docs/INFRA-STAGING-CLOUDFLARE-PAGES.md section 5.
//
// WHAT MUST NOT CHANGE HERE WITHOUT A RULING: "name" (it IS the workers.dev hostname; a different
// name deploys a second Worker at a second URL and the tools point at the wrong one), and
// "directory" (the repo root is the site - every path is relative, exactly as GitHub Pages
// serves release). Files that must never be served are listed in .assetsignore, next to this.
{
  "name": "phantom-staging",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-06-01",
  "assets": {
    "directory": "./",
    "html_handling": "none",
    "not_found_handling": "none"
  }
}
